// A cooperative round-robin scheduler, written entirely in Deed.
//
// The question this file answers is from deed-lang/deed#606: can a scheduler be
// written in Deed, or does something in the handler design prevent it?
//
// The short answer is: yes, with one constraint on how tasks are written.
//
// OCaml's version captures the current continuation when a task yields, puts it
// in the queue, and picks up the next one. Deed handlers are one-shot -- there
// is no continuation to capture -- so that form is not directly available.
//
// What is available is the same structure the compiler already uses for loops:
// a task that wants to yield splits itself into a step that runs now and a
// closure for the rest, then forks the closure before it yields. The scheduler
// never resumes a suspended computation; it just calls the next closure in the
// queue. The tasks here write that split out explicitly.
//
// The concern from deed-lang/deed#292 was about closures inside a handler
// operation capturing the handler's own state. That is not what happens here.
// The closures are written outside the handler entirely and passed in through
// `Schedule.fork`; the handler only stores and calls them. That pattern works
// cleanly.
//
// The three parts that do need to be in the language:
//   - handler state that holds function values (it does)
//   - function values that perform fewer effects than their declared type allows
//     (they do: `a_step2` only uses `Log.note` but satisfies the queue element
//     type which also names `Schedule.fork` and `Schedule.yield`)
//   - a standalone recursive function to drain the queue (ordinary `Diverge`)
//
// Nothing about the scheduler is language machinery.
//
// It is also not a library, and the reason is worth being exact about, because
// "concurrency is a library here" is the claim this file is usually read as
// making. What it shows is that a scheduler can be *written* in Deed. When this
// file was written it could not be *shipped*, and a program that wanted one had
// to copy this file and edit it.
//
// The wall is one line above: `Schedule.fork` takes a
// `Fn() uses Schedule.fork, Schedule.yield, Log.note -> ()`, and that row names
// `Log.note` because these particular tasks log. A library cannot know what its
// tasks will perform, so the queue element type would have to be
// `Fn() uses r -> ()` for a row variable `r` belonging to the effect.
//
// That is what `effect Task<uses r>` is, and `std/task` is the scheduler
// written that way. An effect declares row variables, they are in scope in its
// operation signatures and in the state of any handler that implements it, and
// each `Task.fork` fills the variable in from the value it was passed, so a
// program that forks a task which logs is charged with logging.
// `examples/tasks.deed` is this program written against the library.
//
// This file is kept as it was, because the difference between the two is the
// whole of what a row variable on an effect buys, and it is easier to see side
// by side than described. What is written below still works and still says
// `Log.note` in a type that has nothing to do with logging.

module examples/scheduler

use std/list.{first, drop}

effect Log {
    fn note(message: String) -> ()
    fn messages() -> List<String>
}

handler Recorder implements Log {
    state log: List<String>

    fn note(message) -> () {
        log = push(log, message)
    }

    fn messages() -> List<String> {
        log
    }
}

effect Schedule {
    fn fork(task: Fn() uses Schedule.fork, Schedule.yield, Log.note -> ()) -> ()
    fn yield() -> ()
    fn more() -> Bool
}

handler RoundRobin implements Schedule {
    state queue: List<Fn() uses Schedule.fork, Schedule.yield, Log.note -> ()>

    fn fork(task) -> () {
        queue = push(queue, task)
    }

    fn yield() -> ()
      uses
        Schedule.fork,
        Schedule.yield,
        Log.note,
    {
        match first(queue) {
            ok(task) => {
                queue = drop(queue, 1)
                task()
            },
            err(_) => (),
        }
    }

    fn more() -> Bool {
        length(queue) > 0
    }
}

// Drain the queue. Each call to `Schedule.yield` runs one task, which may
// fork new tasks, so the loop continues until there is nothing left.
fn run() -> ()
  uses
    Schedule.more,
    Schedule.yield,
    Diverge,
{
    if Schedule.more() {
        Schedule.yield()
        run()
    }
}

// Two tasks split into steps. Each step logs that it ran, forks the next step
// for itself, and yields so the other task can proceed.

fn a_step2() -> ()
  uses
    Log.note,
{
    Log.note("A2")
}

fn a_step1() -> ()
  uses
    Schedule.fork,
    Schedule.yield,
    Log.note,
{
    Log.note("A1")
    Schedule.fork(a_step2)
    Schedule.yield()
}

fn b_step2() -> ()
  uses
    Log.note,
{
    Log.note("B2")
}

fn b_step1() -> ()
  uses
    Schedule.fork,
    Schedule.yield,
    Log.note,
{
    Log.note("B1")
    Schedule.fork(b_step2)
    Schedule.yield()
}

test "two tasks interleave round-robin" {
    with Recorder { log: [] }, RoundRobin { queue: [] } {
        Schedule.fork(a_step1)
        Schedule.fork(b_step1)
        run()
        assert Log.messages() == ["A1", "B1", "A2", "B2"]
    }
}

test "tasks queued before run is called are all run" {
    with Recorder { log: [] }, RoundRobin { queue: [] } {
        Schedule.fork(a_step1)
        Schedule.fork(b_step1)
        assert Log.messages() == []
        run()
        assert Log.messages() == ["A1", "B1", "A2", "B2"]
    }
}
